==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|malisoun}}, from {{der|en|fro|maleison}}, from {{der|en|la|maledictiō}}, from the past participle stem of {{m|la|maledīcō||I speak ill of}}, from {{m|la|male||wickedly, badly}} + {{m|la|dīcō||say, speak}}. Compare {{m|en|malediction}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈmælɪzən/|/ˈmælɪsən/}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|obsolete}} A [[curse]], a [[malediction]].
#* {{RQ:Scott Ivanhoe|passage=A hide of land I give to thee in my steads of Walbrugham, from me and mine to thee and thine aye and for ever; and God’s '''malison''' on his head who this gainsays!}}
#* {{RQ:Carlyle French Revolution|text=Rascality male and female is prowling in view of him. His fasting stomach is, with good cause, sour; he perhaps cannot forbear a passing '''malison''' on them; least of all can he forbear answering such.}}

